**Proof.**

Define for $j \geq 1$:
$$S_j = \frac{\prod_{k=0}^{j-1}(1-zx^k)}{\prod_{k=1}^{j-1}(z-x^k)}, \qquad S_1 = 1-z,$$
where the denominator product is empty (equals 1) when $j=1$. Then $P_j = \dfrac{S_j}{z - x^j}$, and the recurrence
$$S_{j+1} = S_j \cdot \frac{1-zx^j}{z-x^j}$$
holds for all $j \geq 1$.

**Key telescoping identity.** We claim
$$(1+x^j)\,P_j = Q_j - Q_{j+1}, \qquad Q_j := \frac{S_j}{z-1}. \tag{$\star$}$$

*Verification.* Using $S_{j+1} = S_j\,\dfrac{1-zx^j}{z-x^j}$:
$$Q_j - Q_{j+1} = \frac{S_j}{z-1} - \frac{S_j(1-zx^j)}{(z-1)(z-x^j)} = \frac{S_j}{z-1}\left[1 - \frac{1-zx^j}{z-x^j}\right] = \frac{S_j}{z-1}\cdot\frac{(z-x^j)-(1-zx^j)}{z-x^j}.$$
The numerator simplifies as $(z-1)+x^j(z-1) = (z-1)(1+x^j)$, so
$$Q_j - Q_{j+1} = \frac{S_j}{z-1}\cdot\frac{(z-1)(1+x^j)}{z-x^j} = \frac{S_j(1+x^j)}{z-x^j} = (1+x^j)\,P_j. \qquad\checkmark$$

**Summing the telescoping series.** For any $N \geq 1$:
$$\sum_{j=1}^{N}(1+x^j)P_j = \sum_{j=1}^{N}(Q_j - Q_{j+1}) = Q_1 - Q_{N+1}.$$
Since $Q_1 = \dfrac{S_1}{z-1} = \dfrac{1-z}{z-1} = -1$, we obtain
$$1 + \sum_{j=1}^{N}(1+x^j)P_j = 1 + (Q_1 - Q_{N+1}) = 1 - 1 - Q_{N+1} = -Q_{N+1}. \tag{$\star\star$}$$

**The tail vanishes.** We now show $Q_{N+1} \to 0$ as $N\to\infty$. Factoring out the $k=0$ term:
$$Q_{N+1} = \frac{1}{z-1}\cdot\frac{\prod_{k=0}^{N}(1-zx^k)}{\prod_{k=1}^{N}(z-x^k)} = \frac{1-z}{z-1}\prod_{k=1}^{N}\frac{1-zx^k}{z-x^k} = -\prod_{k=1}^{N}\frac{1-zx^k}{z-x^k}.$$

Since $|x|<1$, we have $|x^k|\to 0$. Since $|z|>1$, set $\delta = |z|-1>0$ and choose $\varepsilon$ with $0<\varepsilon<\dfrac{|z|-1}{|z|+1}=\dfrac{\delta}{|z|+1}$. Pick $K$ so that $|x^k|<\varepsilon$ for all $k\geq K$. Then for $k\geq K$:
$$\frac{|1-zx^k|}{|z-x^k|} \leq \frac{1+|z|\,|x^k|}{|z|-|x^k|} < \frac{1+|z|\varepsilon}{|z|-\varepsilon} =: r.$$
The choice $\varepsilon < \frac{|z|-1}{|z|+1}$ gives $1+|z|\varepsilon < |z|-\varepsilon$, hence $r<1$. Therefore
$$|Q_{N+1}| = \prod_{k=1}^{N}\frac{|1-zx^k|}{|z-x^k|} = \underbrace{\prod_{k=1}^{K-1}\frac{|1-zx^k|}{|z-x^k|}}_{=:\,C\text{ (finite constant)}} \cdot \prod_{k=K}^{N}\frac{|1-zx^k|}{|z-x^k|} \leq C\,r^{N-K+1}\xrightarrow[N\to\infty]{}0.$$

(The denominators $z-x^k$ are nonzero since $|x^k|<1<|z|$ implies $|x^k|\neq|z|$, so all factors are well-defined.)

**Conclusion.** Taking $N\to\infty$ in ($\star\star$):
$$1 + \sum_{j=1}^{\infty}(1+x^j)P_j = -\lim_{N\to\infty}Q_{N+1} = 0. \qquad\blacksquare$$

